I think the lack of new items is the root cause of most of these.  The iden=
tification game is central to nethack, the wiki even says that it's been ca=
lled the heart of nethack.  As you pointed out the player is pretty much do=
ne with that by the time they get to Gehennom.  Assembling the assention ki=
t is another major part of nethack and is likewise almost over by the time =
the player reaches Gehennom, especially thanks to the castle wand. Taken to=
gether, this means that the designer has little ability to motivate the pla=
yer to do anything but dive for the bottom as quickly as possible. Even if =
there were side branches or special rooms in the mazes, if they're optional=
 it would be hard to get the player to explore them and if they're required=
 it would just be one more (tedious?) obstacle between them and the Sanctum=
.

Addressing those problems, however, would probably require a pretty major r=
e-thinking of the item system in nethack.  So here's my ideas about easier =
ways to add interest to Gehennom:

>  A. Not many major goals you can work on.
>  B. Lots of levels that take a long time to traverse EVERY TIME.
>  C. Lots of nearly-identical levels that take a long time to explore.
>  D. Annoying enemies.  Glob flies, Worf would call them.
>  E. Not enough intesting things to find.
